<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!-- Default locale = English: automatic fallback for unsupported device languages.

     L10n rule of the series, three registers (VISION 1.3, PLANNING Fase 15; full
     record in ../tweather/PLANNING.md Fase 18). The REGISTER decides the language,
     not the punctuation around it:

       CODE  -> always English: YAML/JSON keys, filenames, $ commands, terminal
                output, commit hashes, check lines and verdicts (pass / unstable /
                fail), asserts, levels (ERROR:, WARN:), one-word markers
                (# amended), file banners (# habits.test), licenses.
       DATA  -> localized: nav labels, day names, notification titles, units.
                Habit names are USER data, in whatever language the user writes.
       PROSE -> localized wherever it appears: the README.md day tab, HELP.md,
                $ thabit init, notifications, accessibility text, AND the comment
                lines that are sentences (# tap the command to confirm,
                # empty to turn it off).

     Two tests, in order: would translating it break a lookup, a filename, a
     copy-paste or the alignment with a key printed elsewhere (then it is code)?
     would git translate it (git translates "nothing to commit, working tree clean"
     and never "commit")? One line may hold both registers: keep the tokens and
     translate around them. The comment channel keeps the host file syntax: the
     language changes, the # does not become //.

     This replaces the old wording ("comments stay English"), which mistook the
     channel for the register. It does NOT discharge VISION 3.3.7: a translated
     comment is politeness, not a gloss.

     Implemented in Fase 15. The prose a document decides on travels as a string
     id and the renderer speaks it, so the documents stay pure values and the
     screens stay the only place a locale is read. -->
